# There is no backend/.env — do not create one.
#
# This package has no environment of its own. Every variable it needs
# (DATABASE_URL, SESSION_SECRET, APP_URL, CLEANTRACK_URL, COOKIE_DOMAIN) is
# read from the ONE real file:
#
#   ../frontend/.env.local
#
# That is deliberate, not an oversight — see src/load-env.ts. Next only ever
# reads .env.local from its own directory, and that is what actually runs in
# production. Keeping a second copy here is how a script ends up pointed at a
# different database than the running app, silently, until something breaks
# in a confusing way.
#
# To configure anything, edit ../frontend/.env.local (copy it from
# ../frontend/.env.example if it does not exist yet) and every script in this
# package — db:push, db:bootstrap, db:studio, run from the repository root —
# will pick it up automatically.
#
# If you are looking at this file because you pasted environment variables
# from a hosting dashboard (Render, Vercel) into a file named `backend/.env`:
# stop, delete that file, and put the same values in
# ../frontend/.env.local instead. A stray backend/.env is never read by
# anything, and — worse — it is easy to create it in a form that is not valid
# dotenv syntax (a raw paste of a dashboard's Key/Value table, one per line,
# is NOT `KEY=value` and nothing can parse it), so even a well-intentioned
# copy-paste here silently does nothing.
